            <ArticleTitle>Phlebosclerotic colitis: value of radiography in diagnosis--report of three cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>188-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Three cases sharing the following radiologic features are reported: (a) abdominal conventional radiography-vascular calcifications at the right hemicolon, (b) abdominal computed tomography-colonic wall thickening and venous calcifications, and (c) barium enema examination-luminal narrowing of the right hemicolon and thumbprinting. There were no clinical or laboratory findings suggestive of portal hypertension. The disease entity, &quot;phlebosclerotic colitis,&quot; should be differentiated from ordinary ischemic colitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
